<!-- v3 (2026-11-17, help overhaul): John's structure (intro, What it does,
     How to use this tool, then more) and the shared blocks in ui/css/help.css. -->
<p>What fun is a large music library if you can't share tracks with friends?</p>

<h3>What it does:</h3>
<p>Music Sharing turns an MP3 and its album cover into a square MP4 video, the format social media sites accept.</p>

<section class="steps">
    <h3>How to use this tool:</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Choose <span class="ui-button">Choose a track…</span>, then find the MP3 in your library. Its title, artist, album and length appear, with a <strong>Preview</strong> of the video beside them.</li>
        <li>Choose <span class="ui-button">Make the video</span> (or press Enter). The bar shows how far along it is. <span class="ui-button">Stop</span> ends it and saves nothing.</li>
        <li>When it's finished, <span class="ui-button">Show the video</span> opens your Videos folder with the new file selected, ready to share.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">The music in your files usually still belongs to its publisher. Share on social media at your own risk.</p>
</section>

<h3>What's in the video</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Picture:</strong> the cover stored in the MP3. If there isn't one, <code>folder.jpg</code> from the album folder. If there's neither, a plain black square. The picture is cropped to fill a 1080 × 1080 square.</li>
    <li><strong>Caption:</strong> "Audio taken from my personal library", laid over the picture. The preview shows exactly how it will look.</li>
    <li><strong>Sound:</strong> the whole track, as 192k AAC, with H.264 video.</li>
    <li><strong>Song details:</strong> the title, artist, album and year are copied into the MP4, so players show them. Nothing else from the MP3 is carried over.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Where videos are saved</h3>
<p>In your Videos folder, under <code>MetaForge Studio</code>, named after the MP3. <span class="ui-button">Open the videos folder</span> takes you there at any time.</p>
<p>If a video with that name is already there, MetaForge asks whether to <span class="ui-button">Replace it</span> or <span class="ui-button">Keep both</span> (the new one gets a number, such as "(2)").</p>

<h3>If something goes wrong</h3>
<p>A video is only saved once it's complete and the same length as the track. A video that fails or comes out short is thrown away, never left half-made. If you switch tools while a video is being made, MetaForge asks first.</p>
